\section{Computational complexity and system design}

We summarize per-decision-step asymptotics (with $b$ average branching factor, $d$ depth, $m$ threads, $M$ population size, $K$ checks, and $|M|$ memory slots). These are worst-case bounds ignoring constant-factor tokenization overheads:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=0.2em]
\item IGD: computing indices over $m$ threads requires posterior updates and a bounded-horizon lookahead; with amortized constant-time updates per step, $O(m)$. Risk-LCBs add $O(m)$ variance estimates. Memory $O(m)$ for thread state.
\item RS-MCTT: UCT-style selection/backup $O(b)$ per step with rollouts $O(d)$; total per node $O(b + d)$. Risk transforms use log-moment estimates, $O(1)$ per update. Memory proportional to visited nodes, $\tilde{O}(b^d)$ in worst case with substantial empirical pruning.
\item ADR: information-gain computation depends on check cost; for $K$ candidate checks, $O(K)$ scoring with cached posteriors.
\item CSC: constraint checks and message passing across $k$ chains: $O(kK)$ per round; majority voting $O(k)$.
\item DRSM: surplus computation across subproblems $|\mathcal{P}|$ is $O(|\mathcal{P}|)$; dual updates (e.g., ADMM) add $O(|\mathcal{P}|)$ per iteration \cite{boyd2011admm}.
\item BB-LLM: branch-and-bound is exponential in the worst case but prunes substantially with admissible bounds; per-node evaluation is dominated by heuristic computation and verifier calls.
\item APT: per epoch mutation/recombination $O(M)$; reweighting $O(M)$; resampling $O(M)$; diversity control adds negligible overhead.
\item DEMI: write selection with submodular gains is $O(|M|)$ greedy per step with $(1-1/e)$ approximation \cite{sviridenko2004}; retrieval/indexing cost depends on key-value store; locality-sensitive hashing typically $\tilde{O}(1)$ expected per query.
\item PSV: $O(K)$ likelihood updates; stopping via EVC-threshold is $O(1)$ once summaries are maintained.
\item DPG: gating decision $O(1)$ per example; escalation triggers the selected algorithm’s cost.
\end{itemize}
System design includes (i) a scheduler (EVC or indices), (ii) a search/planning module (e.g., RS-MCTT), (iii) verifiers and checks (PSV/BB-LLM), and (iv) ephemeral memory and retrieval (DEMI). Interfaces among modules pass scores, posteriors, and constraints rather than raw text, enabling calibrated and amortized decision rules.

\section{Psychology and neuroscience links}

The proposed algorithms mirror aspects of human deliberation. 
IGD parallels \emph{rational metareasoning} and selective attention to promising lines of thought \cite{liedergriffiths2020}. 
RS-MCTT resonates with \emph{risk-sensitive control} and flexible exploration--exploitation \cite{howard1972risk}. 
ADR formalizes \emph{hypothesis testing} and the role of \emph{falsification}. 
APT reflects population-based cognition and \emph{global workspace} accumulation of evidence \cite{dehaene2011gnw}. 
DEMI aligns with \emph{working memory} and gating by prefrontal mechanisms \cite{baddeley1992workingmemory,gershmandaw2017}. 
Replay-like search in RS-MCTT and APT connects to hippocampal \emph{preplay} and \emph{vicarious trial and error} \cite{pfeifferfoster2013}.

\section{Evaluation without experiments: what to measure}

To compare test-time compute algorithms abstractly:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=0.25em]
\item \textbf{Frontier area (FA):} $\int_0^{B_{\max}} P(B)\, dB$ approximated by discrete budgets; larger area indicates better use of compute across scales.
\item \textbf{Marginal value-of-compute (MVC):} $\Delta P/\Delta B$ near operational budgets; steeper MVC is preferable.
\item \textbf{Reliability at fixed budget:} Error or risk under RS-MCTT/DPG-type controls; include ECE and Brier decomposition by confidence bins.
\item \textbf{Diversity efficiency:} Quality gain per \emph{independent} reasoning dimension (APT/CSC).
\item \textbf{Verifier yield:} Utility gain per unit of verification compute (PSV/BB-LLM).
\item \textbf{Memory return:} Improvement per memory operation (DEMI).
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion}

Our results emphasize that compute \emph{allocation} matters as much as compute \emph{quantity}. The BPF concavity highlights diminishing returns; thus, early-budget improvements (high MVC) are particularly valuable for latency-constrained or cost-sensitive settings. IGD’s index policy prioritizes the most promising reasoning thread given uncertainty and cost, while RS-MCTT’s risk annealing curbs brittle branches near decision time. These mechanisms, compatible with CoT/ToT generation, rationalize observed empirical gains.

Limitations remain. First, local EVC estimates and self-evaluations may be miscalibrated; explicit calibration (ECE/Brier) and verified checks help mitigate, but calibration itself requires careful design. Second, our synthetic setting abstracts away domain-specific constraints; real-world benchmarks (GSM8K, MATH, HumanEval) are needed to validate generality and inform practical hyperparameter schedules (e.g., index priors, risk temperatures). Third, population methods (APT) and markets (DRSM) can inherit biases from scoring functions; mitigation includes counterfactual checks (CSC), debiasing in scoring, and audit trails.

Practically, we recommend: (i) deploying DPG to gate into structured deliberation only when needed; (ii) using IGD or RS-MCTT as the primary scheduler; (iii) incorporating PSV-style checks where weak verifiers exist; (iv) monitoring MVC and FA to decide budget schedules; and (v) instrumenting logs to estimate calibration and guide future tuning. As models evolve, the metareasoning layer can be reused, improving amortized deployment without retraining base LLMs.

\section{Related work}
Prompted reasoning traces such as CoT \cite{wei2022cot} and self-consistency \cite{wang2023selfconsistency} improve LLM problem-solving but lack formal compute allocation. 
Tree-of-thought search \cite{yao2023tot} and debate-style multi-agent prompting \cite{irving2018debate} add structure but often use fixed schedules. 
Our framework builds on metareasoning \cite{russellwefald1991,horvitz1989,liedergriffiths2020}, submodular optimization \cite{nemhauser1978,golovin2011adaptive}, risk-sensitive control \cite{howard1972risk,ahmadi2012entropic}, and bandit indices \cite{gittins1979,whittle1988restless}, and connects them to LLM inference and memory-augmented modeling \cite{weston2015memnn,graves2016dnc,lewis2020rag}, as well as prompt strategies like least-to-most \cite{zhou2022leasttomost}, ReAct \cite{yao2022react}, and PAL \cite{gao2023pal}.

\section{Limitations and societal considerations}

The theory presumes access to calibrated self-evaluations and verifiers; miscalibration may misallocate compute or prematurely stop. 
Risk-sensitive search mitigates hallucination but may be conservative on creative tasks. 
Market-like and multi-agent procedures (DRSM, APT) can amplify biases if scoring functions are biased. 
Responsible deployment should pair compute-scaling with audit trails, verifiers, and explicit stopping policies.

